{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1648 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "d7997db5954763462fc1507b9801a7e96aba686590ba0eaf6afd00e5c484e43f" :edb/db-before-t 1647 :edb/db-after-t 1648 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534960 50 #inst "2026-10-08T12:11:40.979Z" 13194139534960 true] [17592186087391 1100 "Use the transformation $y = vx$  (where $v=v(x)$) to rewrite the given first-order homogeneous ODE \n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}    =   \\cos \\left( \\frac {y} {x} \\right)\n$$\n\nas a separable ODE in $v$ and $x.$" 13194139534960 true] [17592186087391 1100 "Use the transformation $y = vx$ (where $v=v(x)$) to rewrite the given first-order homogeneous ODE\n$\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = \\cos \\left(\\frac {y} {x} \\right)$\nas a separable ODE in $v$ and $x$." 13194139534960 false] [17592186087391 1145 "With $y = vx$ (where $v = v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging that into the differential equation and separating variables, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v)-v \\\\ (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(v)-v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534960 true] [17592186087391 1145 "With $y = vx$ (where $v = v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx} = x\\frac{dv}{dx} + v\n$$\n\nPlugging that into the differential equation and separating variables, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\cos (\\frac{y}{x}) \\\\\nx\\frac{dv}{dx} + v &= \\cos (v) \\\\\nx\\frac{dv}{dx} &= \\cos (v) - v \\\\\n((1)/(\\cos (v) - v))\\frac{dv}{dx} &= \\frac{1}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534960 false] [17592186087392 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/12aec388c94c8c4d4f2099b98f5a489d110fa3ef0d58e7895d9a425e49f8eb77.png)" 13194139534960 true] [17592186087392 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\frac{x^2+y^2}{2xy}$." 13194139534960 false] [17592186087392 1145 "We note that this equation is a homogeneous first-order differential equation because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(tx, ty) &= ((tx)^{2} + (ty)^{2})/(2(tx)(ty)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{t^{2}x^{2} + t^{2}y^{2}}{2t^{2}y} \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{2} + y^{2}}{2xy} \\\\\n&= f(x, y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given equation is not separable, but we can transform it into a separable equation by carrying out the substitution\n\n$$\ny=vx, \\quad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = v + x \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} \\,\n$$\n\nCarrying out the substitution and separating variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{x^{2} + y^{2}}{2xy} \\\\\nv + x\\frac{dv}{dx} &= (x^{2} + (vx)^{2})/(2x(vx)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{2} + v^{2}x^{2}}{2x^{2}v} \\\\\n&= (x^{2}(1 + v^{2}))/(2x^{2}v) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 + v^{2}}{2v} \\\\\nx\\frac{dv}{dx} &= \\frac{1 + v^{2}}{2v} - v \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 + v^{2}}{2v} - \\frac{2v^{2}}{2v} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - v^{2}}{2v} \\\\\n\\frac{2v}{1 - v^{2}}\\frac{dv}{dx} &= \\frac{1}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{2v}{1 - v^{2}}\\frac{dv}{dx}dx &= ∫\\frac{1}{x}dx \\\\\n∫\\frac{2v}{1 - v^{2}}dv &= \\ln\\begin{vmatrix}x & + C_{1} \\\\ -\\ln & 1 - v^{2}\\end{vmatrix}|= \\ln \\mid C_{2}x \\mid \\\\\n\\ln\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{1}{1 - v^{2}} & &= \\ln & C_{2}x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{1 - v^{2}} &= C_{2}x \\\\\n1 - v^{2} &= \\frac{1}{C_{2}x} \\\\\n1 - v^{2} &= \\frac{C_{3}}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nC_1=\\ln{C_2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{C_2}=C_3\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting\n\n$$\nv=\\dfrac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nand simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 - v^{2} &= \\frac{C_{3}}{x} \\\\\n1 - (\\frac{y}{x})^{2} &= \\frac{C_{3}}{x} \\\\\n1 - \\frac{y^{2}}{x^{2}} &= \\frac{C_{3}}{x} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{x^{2}} &= 1 - \\frac{C_{3}}{x} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{x^{2}} &= 1 + \\frac{C_{4}}{x} \\\\\ny^{2} &= x^{2}(1 + \\frac{C_{4}}{x}) \\\\\ny^{2} &= x^{2} + C_{4}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_4 = -C_3$. Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= x^{2} + C_{4}x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534960 false] [17592186087392 1145 "We note that this equation is a homogeneous first-order differential equation because\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/c3215663fd1a332f750b8af4049404061e2b1c64726c81572d1a1e76816bb718.png)\n\nThe given equation is not separable, but we can transform it into a separable equation by carrying out the substitution\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/7c57d5a7c4003269ba9eacf6240394f508347989a9d75665c7070e31cbdad701.png)\n\nCarrying out the substitution and separating variables, we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/8ad7a31876bc67fa69ff24ed41badcf6531ddf52b2d1847af4b0b75595ab5bcd.png)\n\nIntegrating both sides, we get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/7a6577d6ac4f3f1fc100a2767d77509fbe211f7d3e7bc7a2bba027080e7e6ccd.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/4247e7a16afb46979bc6ecfc9ae10dc6c92d0f5f1ff5e1b2a1f412ec86a48a83.png) (with ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/9e71f72815a80396d1186bfed1047662e1d507561c7160d1c2d89c2f23421c09.png)) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/012dd077bd0f68f82ae37fa50d493e97bd67ac5ff9f301e48ca29e91b1af16ab.png) \n\nFinally, substituting ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/f74fcd854fc4be51cd8eed8f4d0649bcc7cf11089a7aa2a7e7edd8d306693f78.png) and simplifying, we get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/05eae130ba10a3386a1c8621ecc3e3d3ec0bb7af352fb4c928cf8bf2d88c21a3.png)\nwhere ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/8939a3ee49356a0cc8515cce98d84ecf10279dad9d2f76fac07660d0f99a0d7b.png) Therefore, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044192/assets/185e5a72b055183d70fb6d9dbc0227190a8114b80b2dc4193d8f542d5c85c0d7.png)" 13194139534960 true] [17592186087393 1100 "Which of the following differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{x}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}$" 13194139534960 true] [17592186087393 1100 "Which of the following differential equations are homogeneous?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{x}$\n2. $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$\n3. $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}$" 13194139534960 false] [17592186087393 1145 "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    f(x, y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero, meaning that it satisfies\n\n$$\nf(tx, ty) = f(x, y).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at the right-hand side function in each differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation I is a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$ is *not* a homogeneous function of degree $0.$ Rather, it is a homogeneous function of degree $-1\\mathbin{:}$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{{t}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}f(x,y)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation II is *not* a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{ty}{2(tx)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot 2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation III is a homogenous differential equation.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534960 true] [17592186087393 1145 "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} = f(x, y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero, meaning that it satisfies\n\n$$\nf(tx, ty) = f(x, y)\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at the right-hand side function in each differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n$f(tx, ty)|= ((ty))/((tx)); = \\frac{t \\cdot y}{t \\cdot x}; = \\frac{y}{x}; = f(x, y)✓$\nTherefore, equation I is a homogenous differential equation.\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$ is *not* a homogeneous function of degree $0$. Rather, it is a homogeneous function of degree $-1$:\n$f(tx, ty)|= ((ty))/((tx)^{2}); = \\frac{t \\cdot y}{t^{2} \\cdot x^{2}}; = \\frac{1}{t} \\cdot \\frac{y}{x^{2}}; = \\frac{1}{t} \\cdot f(x, y); = t^{-1}f(x, y)\\cdot$\nTherefore, equation II is *not* a homogenous differential equation.\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n$f(tx, ty)|= (ty)/(2(tx))\\sin (((tx)^{2})/((ty)^{2})); = \\frac{t \\cdot y}{t \\cdot 2x}\\sin (\\frac{t^{2}x^{2}}{t^{2}y^{2}}); = \\frac{y}{2x}\\sin (\\frac{x^{2}}{y^{2}}); = f(x, y)✓$\nTherefore, equation III is a homogenous differential equation.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534960 false] [17592186087825 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322706 13194139534960 true] [17592186087825 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322713 13194139534960 true] [17592186087825 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322747 13194139534960 true] [17592186087826 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322727 13194139534960 true] [17592186087826 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322761 13194139534960 true] [17592186087826 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322768 13194139534960 true] [17592186087826 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322775 13194139534960 true] [17592186087827 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322720 13194139534960 true] [17592186087827 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322734 13194139534960 true] [17592186087827 1020 #edb/ref 17592186322754 13194139534960 true] [17592186087947 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534960 true] [17592186087947 1100 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+y{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" 13194139534960 true] [17592186087947 1100 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{3y^{3}}{xy^{2} + yx^{2}}$.\n\nIf the transformation $y = v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" 13194139534960 false] [17592186087947 1145 "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{y}^{3}}{x{y}^{2}+y{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3(vx)^{3}}{x(vx)^{2}+(vx){x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3{v}^{3}{x}^{3}}{v{x}^{3}(v+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{3{v}^{3}\\cancel{{x}^{3}}}{v\\cancel{{x}^{3}}(v+1)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}}{v+1}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}}{v+1}-\\frac{v(v+1)}{v+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3{v}^{2}-{v}^{2}-v}{v+1} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2{v}^{2}-v}{v+1} \\\\ (\\frac{v+1}{2{v}^{2}-v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534960 true] [17592186088381 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534960 true] [17592186088381 1145 "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$\n\nWe see that both the numerator and denominator are homogeneous polynomials of degree $2.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $2-2=0.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{2(tx)(ty)}{(tx)^{2}+(ty)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}\\cdot 2xy}{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{{t}^{2}\\cdot 2xy}{{t}^{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is homogeneous." 13194139534960 true] [17592186088382 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534960 true] [17592186088382 1145 "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is NOT the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=x.$\n\nWe see that it is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $1.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is  NOT homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\,\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is not homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =tx=tf(x,y)\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is NOT homogeneous." 13194139534960 true] [17592186088383 1017 #edb/ref 17592186087228 13194139534960 false] [17592186088383 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322741 13194139534960 true] [17592186088383 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534960 true] [17592186088383 1100 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})+\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" 13194139534960 true] [17592186088383 1100 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\sec (\\frac{x}{y}) + \\frac{y}{x}$.\n\nIf the transformation $y = v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" 13194139534960 false] [17592186088383 1145 "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{y})+\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{x}{vx})+\\frac{vx}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})+v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{1}{v}) \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534960 true] [17592186088388 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534960 true] [17592186088388 1100 "Find the general solution of the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{xy}\n$$" 13194139534960 true] [17592186088388 1100 "Find the general solution of the following differential equation.\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{y^{2} - x^{2}}{xy}$" 13194139534960 false] [17592186088388 1145 "This is a homogeneous equation because it is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y),\n$$\n\nand we notice that all terms in the numerator and denominator of $f(x,y)$ are of order $2$ (i.e., they are quadratic).\n\nUsing the transformation $y=v(x)x$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{xy} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{(vx)^{2}-{x}^{2}}{x(vx)} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\frac{{x}^{2}({v}^{2}-1)}{v{x}^{2}} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{2}-1}{v}-v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{v}^{2}-1-{v}^{2}}{v} \\\\ v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int v\\,\\text{d}v & =-\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{{v}^{2}}{2} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+2{C}_{1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we introduced $C=2{C}_{1}.$\n\nFrom the transformation $y=vx$, we get\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{y}{x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above into our solution, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{y}{x})}^{2} & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+C \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}+C{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534960 true] [17592186322706 1017 #edb/ref 17592186322707 13194139534960 true] [17592186322706 1089 "q-14314" 13194139534960 true] [17592186322706 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534960 true] [17592186322706 1100 "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}})+\\frac{3y}{x}.\n$$\n\nIf the transformation $y=v(x)x$ is applied to the differential equation, what is the resulting equation?" 13194139534960 true] [17592186322706 1113 #uuid "de0afd56-302d-5546-b6ff-08ceea918dd3" 13194139534960 true] [17592186322706 1145 "Using the transformation $y=vx$ (where $v=v(x)$), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given differential equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}})+\\frac{3y}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{v}^{2}{x}^{2}})+\\frac{3vx}{x} \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}+v & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+3v \\\\ x\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+2v \\\\ (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+2v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534960 true] [17592186322707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322708 13194139534960 true] [17592186322707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322709 13194139534960 true] [17592186322707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322710 13194139534960 true] [17592186322707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322711 13194139534960 true] [17592186322707 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322712 13194139534960 true] [17592186322707 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534960 true] [17592186322707 1041 "selection" 13194139534960 true] [17592186322707 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322709 13194139534960 true] [17592186322707 1110 #uuid "2c35e0ee-e673-5d29-81a2-1d81a15b0156" 13194139534960 true] [17592186322708 1034 "(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{v})+v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" 13194139534960 true] [17592186322708 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534960 true] [17592186322708 1111 #uuid "98e21887-70b6-5d9f-a59a-65c42892d51a" 13194139534960 true] [17592186322709 1034 "(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{1}{{v}^{2}})+2v})\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}" 13194139534960 true] [17592186322709 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534960 true] [17592