2186322754 1145 "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is NOT the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}.$\n\nWe see that it is a homogeneous polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $2.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is  NOT homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is not homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}f(x,y)\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)\\ne f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is NOT homogeneous." 13194139534960 true] [17592186322755 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322756 13194139534960 true] [17592186322755 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322757 13194139534960 true] [17592186322755 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322758 13194139534960 true] [17592186322755 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322759 13194139534960 true] [17592186322755 1000 #edb/ref 17592186322760 13194139534960 true] [17592186322755 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534960 true] [17592186322755 1041 "selection" 13194139534960 true] [17592186322755 1104 #edb/ref 17592186322759 13194139534960 true] [17592186322755 1110 #uuid "2dba84e5-66b0-51da-b273-8ce153009b18" 13194139534960 true] [17592186322756 1034 "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+xy}" 13194139534960 true] [17592186322756 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534960 true] [17592186322756 1111 #uuid "91c4cf49-08d9-5786-9604-ed9b592f0f25" 13194139534960 true] [17592186322757 10